From 4c228bb447951ee1b7532c9baae6c73f8dbaf803 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 09:10:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 10/11] kernelbase: WINE_FAST_MUTEX=1: unnamed mutexes taken
 and released in user space

Under WoW64 with an emulated x86 CPU a WaitForSingleObject() +
ReleaseMutex() pair costs ~2.4 us on the GO Ultra: two system calls,
each through Box64, wow64, ntdll and ntsync. Sid Meier's Pirates! wraps
every small read of its pack files in such a pair: ~23000 of them when a
ship comes into view at sea, ~100 ms of a 210 ms frame.

With WINE_FAST_MUTEX=1 in the environment, the unnamed, non-inheritable
mutexes of the program live in user space: taking and releasing one is
an atomic operation, 0.2-0.3 us. The kernel mutex is still created and
stays unowned while the fast path runs it. A thread that has to wait
sleeps on an auto-reset event set by releases while someone waits;
WaitAny of fast mutexes and other objects waits for both. Waits that
cannot be done there (WaitAll, alertable ones, a duplicate for another
process, RegisterWaitForSingleObjectEx) hand the mutex over to the
kernel for good, with its owner's ownership when the owner asks, else
once it is free. Waiters look every 500 ms whether the owner died owning
it and then get it as abandoned.

Opt-in per program: MsgWaitForMultipleObjects(), kernel32's
RegisterWaitForSingleObject(), SetThreadpoolWait() and direct ntdll calls
do not know fast mutexes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
